\title{One-Sidedness of Conditional-Independence Artifacts}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{8 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Empirical dependence tables are almost never computed on the true alphabet of
the explanatory variable. Label collisions --- hash truncations, schema
rebuilds, coarse categorical encodings --- pool distinct settings into a single
reported row. We determine the exact effect of such collisions on conditional
readings. Our starting point is a conditional data-processing inequality valid
for \emph{arbitrary nonnegative weights}, not merely probability measures: for
every label map $f$ and every weight on $X\times Y\times Z$, $I(f(X);Y\mid Z)
\le I(X;Y\mid Z)$, with equality for injective $f$. Consequently every collision
artifact in a conditional dependence table is a false negative; a reported
conditional dependence is always genuine. We then upgrade the inequality to an
identity: the information destroyed by a merge equals, exactly, the sum over
conditioning contexts and over fibers of the merge of the marginal fiber deficit
minus the sliced fiber deficits, localising every discrepancy to a single
(context, fiber) pair. Pursuing the equality case yields a complete
classification: a merged conditional reading is exact if and only if each fiber
is a product block in every context of positive mass --- equivalently, the
pooled settings share a normalised response profile --- and is strictly smaller
otherwise. We supplement the sign guarantee with a computable magnitude
guarantee: the loss is bounded by the label entropy the merge destroys, a
functional of the encoding and the marginal dial counts alone, and the bound is
attained. We further show that merges compose, so the sign guarantee is stable
under chaining; that readings are capped by $H(X\mid Z)$; and that a chain rule
$I(X;Y\mid Z)=I(X;(Y,Z))-I(X;Z)$ reduces the audit of a conditional column to
two ordinary two-variable readings. Explicit $2\times2\times2$ witnesses delimit
the theory: conditional and unconditional readings are logically independent,
and the sign guarantee fails outright when the conditioning variable, rather
than the explanatory variable, is merged.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} conditional mutual information; data-processing
inequality; label merge; entropy deficit; one-sided error; collision artifact;
Gibbs' inequality; superadditivity.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Consider a dependence table indexed by the settings of some explanatory
variable --- a routing rule, a configuration dial, a treatment arm --- reporting
for each context whether that variable influences an observed response. Such a
table is read as a source of claims of the form ``in context $z$, the dial
influences the response'' or ``in context $z$, it does not.''

In any realistic pipeline the dial alphabet reaching the table is a coarsening
of the true one. Identifiers are truncated to a fixed width; a hash collides; a
schema migration folds several old categories into one new one; a compound key
is logged only in part. Formally, the observed table is the pushforward of the
true joint weight along some label map $f$, and distinct true settings $x_1\neq
x_2$ with $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$ have had their rows added together.

The question we answer is not whether the resulting table is correct --- it
generally is not --- but \emph{in which direction it can be wrong}, by how much,
and under exactly which circumstances it is nonetheless right.

\subsection{Results}

Write $I(X;Y\mid Z)$ for the conditional mutual information of the dial and the
response given the context (Section~\ref{sec:setting} makes all definitions
precise for unnormalised weights).

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \textbf{One-sidedness} (Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi}). $I(f(X);Y\mid Z)\le
I(X;Y\mid Z)$ for every label map and every nonnegative weight, with equality
for injective $f$. Hence a reported conditional dependence is never a false
positive.
\item \textbf{Exact accounting} (Theorem~\ref{thm:cmiloss}). The loss is a
double sum over contexts and fibers of marginal-minus-sliced deficits; a single
positive term forces a strict drop.
\item \textbf{Classification} (Theorem~\ref{thm:classify}). Equality holds iff
every fiber is a product block in every context of positive mass.
\item \textbf{Observable error bar} (Theorem~\ref{thm:errorbar}). The loss is at
most the label entropy destroyed, computable without the joint table; the bound
is attained.
\item \textbf{Chaining and structure} (Section~\ref{sec:structure}). Merges
compose; readings are monotone along a chain; sub-dial readings are lower
bounds; $I(X;Y\mid Z)\le H(X\mid Z)$.
\item \textbf{Chain rule} (Theorem~\ref{thm:chainrule}). $I(X;Y\mid
Z)=I(X;(Y,Z))-I(X;Z)$.
\item \textbf{Sharpness and limits} (Section~\ref{sec:witnesses}). Conditional
and unconditional readings are logically independent; merges of the conditioning
variable are two-sided.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{What is and is not standard}

The unconditional data-processing inequality is classical. Three features
distinguish the development below. \emph{Unnormalised weights:} everything is
stated for arbitrary nonnegative weights, with no assumption that the total mass
is $1$, which forces an explicit mass correction in the definition of the
conditional reading. \emph{Exactness rather than inequality:} the centre of
gravity is the identity of Theorem~\ref{thm:cmiloss} and the classification of
Theorem~\ref{thm:classify}. \emph{Audit orientation:} the error bar of
Theorem~\ref{thm:errorbar} is expressed in a quantity an auditor can compute
\emph{without access to the true joint table}.

\section{Setting and definitions}\label{sec:setting}

Throughout, $X$, $Y$, $Z$ denote finite sets: the \emph{dial} alphabet, the
\emph{response} alphabet and the \emph{context} alphabet. All logarithms are
base $2$ and all information quantities are in bits.

\begin{definition}[Shannon term]
For $w\ge0$ set $\nlp(w)=-w\log_2 w$, with $\nlp(0)=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Entropy of a weight]
For a finite set $S$ and $w:S\to\R$, $H(S,w)=\sum_{x\in S}\nlp(w(x))$. No
normalisation is assumed; for a nonnegative weight of total mass $M>0$ one has
$H(S,w)=M\,H(S,w/M)+\nlp(M)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fiber deficit]
For $S$ finite and $w:S\to\R_{\ge0}$,
\[
D(S,w)\;=\;H(S,w)-\nlp\!\Big(\sum_{x\in S}w(x)\Big)
\;=\;\sum_{x\in S}w(x)\log_2\frac{\sum_{x'\in S}w(x')}{w(x)}.
\]
This is the entropy destroyed by pooling the block $S$ into a single label. By
Gibbs' inequality $D(S,w)\ge0$, with equality iff at most one element of $S$
carries positive weight.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Two-way tables]
For $q:X\times Y\to\R$ put $q_1(x)=\sum_y q(x,y)$, $q_2(y)=\sum_x q(x,y)$,
$\mass(q)=\sum_{x,y}q(x,y)$, and
\[
I(q)=H(X,q_1)+H(Y,q_2)-H(X\times Y,q).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Slices and the conditional reading]
For $p:X\times Y\times Z\to\R$ and $z\in Z$ the \emph{slice} is
$p_z(x,y)=p(x,y,z)$. The \emph{conditional reading} is
\[
I(X;Y\mid Z)\;:=\;\sum_{z\in Z}\Big[I(p_z)-\nlp\big(\mass(p_z)\big)\Big].
\]
For a probability weight this reduces to $\sum_{x,y,z}p(x,y,z)\log_2
\frac{p(x,y,z)p(z)}{p(x,z)p(y,z)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Label merges]
A \emph{label map} is any function $f:X\to X'$ into a finite set, with
\emph{fiber} $\fib{f}{u}=\{x\in X: f(x)=u\}$. The \emph{merge} of a three-way
weight is the pushforward in the first coordinate,
\[
(f_*p)(u,y,z)=\sum_{x\in \fib{f}{u}}p(x,y,z),
\]
and analogously $(f_*q)(u,y)$ for two-way tables; we write $\push_f w(u)=
\sum_{x\in \fib{f}{u}}w(x)$ for one-way weights.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Compatibilities]\label{lem:compat}
For all $f$, $p$, $z$:
(i) $(f_*p)_z=f_*(p_z)$;
(ii) $\mass(f_*q)=\mass(q)$;
(iii) $(f_*q)_2=q_2$ and $(f_*q)_1=\push_f(q_1)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
All three are Fubini for finite sums together with the fact that the fibers of
$f$ partition $X$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Deficit identities]\label{lem:deficit}
Let $w:X\to\R_{\ge0}$ and $q:X\times Y\to\R_{\ge0}$. Then
\[
H(X,w)-H(X',\push_f w)=\sum_{u\in X'}D\big(\fib{f}{u},w\big),
\]
\[
H(X\times Y,q)-H(X'\times Y,f_*q)
=\sum_{u\in X'}\sum_{y\in Y}D\big(\fib{f}{u},q(\cdot,y)\big).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both express that $H$ over a set partitioned into fibers splits as the entropy
of the pooled weights plus the within-fiber deficits, the second applied column
by column.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Superadditivity of the deficit]\label{lem:super}
For $v_y:S\to\R_{\ge0}$, $y\in Y$, and any block $S\subseteq X$,
\[
\sum_{y\in Y}D(S,v_y)\;\le\;D\Big(S,\ \sum_{y\in Y}v_y\Big).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Concavity of $w\mapsto H(S,w)$; equivalently the log-sum inequality applied
columnwise.
\end{proof}

\section{One-sidedness}

\begin{proposition}[Nonnegativity]\label{prop:nonneg}
For every nonnegative $q$ on $X\times Y$ we have $I(q)-\nlp(\mass(q))\ge0$, and
hence $I(X;Y\mid Z)\ge0$ for every nonnegative $p$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix a slice $q$ of mass $M$. If $M=0$ every term vanishes. Otherwise apply
Gibbs' inequality to $q$ against the product comparison weight
$b(x,y)=q_1(x)q_2(y)/M$, which is nonnegative, absolutely continuous with
respect to $q$ (if $b(x,y)=0$ a marginal vanishes, and $q(x,y)\le
\min(q_1(x),q_2(y))$ forces $q(x,y)=0$), and has total mass exactly $M$.
Expanding $\log_2 b(x,y)=\log_2 q_1(x)+\log_2 q_2(y)-\log_2 M$ and resumming
turns the relative entropy into precisely $I(q)-\nlp(M)$. Summing over $z$ gives
the conditional statement.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional data-processing inequality]\label{thm:dpi}
For every label map $f:X\to X'$ and every nonnegative weight $p$ on $X\times
Y\times Z$,
\[
I(f(X);Y\mid Z)\;\le\;I(X;Y\mid Z).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:compat} the $z$-slice of the merged table is the merge of the
$z$-slice and has the same mass, so the mass corrections agree slice by slice
and it suffices to prove $I(f_*q)\le I(q)$ for a single slice. Using
Lemma~\ref{lem:compat}(iii),
\[
I(q)-I(f_*q)=\big[H(q_1)-H(\push_f q_1)\big]-\big[H(q)-H(f_*q)\big],
\]
which by Lemma~\ref{lem:deficit} equals
$\sum_u D(\fib{f}{u},q_1)-\sum_u\sum_y D(\fib{f}{u},q(\cdot,y))$. This is
nonnegative term by term by Lemma~\ref{lem:super} with $v_y=q(\cdot,y)$, since
$\sum_y q(\cdot,y)=q_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Encoding invariance]\label{thm:inj}
If $f$ is injective then $I(f(X);Y\mid Z)=I(X;Y\mid Z)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every fiber is a singleton, so every deficit in the display above vanishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Reconciliation of two encodings]
If $f$ is injective and $g$ arbitrary then $I(g(X);Y\mid Z)\le I(f(X);Y\mid Z)$:
a width-valid encoding reports the true conditional value and any other encoding
reports at most that value.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[One-sided error]\label{cor:onesided}
\emph{(i)} If $I(f(X);Y\mid Z)>0$ then $I(X;Y\mid Z)>0$: a reported conditional
dependence is never a false positive. \emph{(ii)} If $I(X;Y\mid Z)=0$ then
$I(f(X);Y\mid Z)=0$ for every $f$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
(i) is immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi}; (ii) combines
Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} with Proposition~\ref{prop:nonneg} applied to the merged
table.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact accounting}

\begin{theorem}[Unconditional loss identity]\label{thm:miloss}
For every label map $f$ and every weight $q$ on $X\times Y$,
\[
I(q)-I(f_*q)=\sum_{u\in X'}D\big(\fib{f}{u},q_1\big)
-\sum_{u\in X'}\sum_{y\in Y}D\big(\fib{f}{u},q(\cdot,y)\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine both parts of Lemma~\ref{lem:deficit} with
Lemma~\ref{lem:compat}(iii); the response marginal contributes identically to
$I(q)$ and $I(f_*q)$ and cancels.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional loss identity]\label{thm:cmiloss}
For every $f$ and every weight $p$,
\[
I(X;Y\mid Z)-I(f(X);Y\mid Z)=\sum_{z\in Z}\sum_{u\in X'}\Delta(z,u),
\]
\[
\Delta(z,u):=D\big(\fib{f}{u},p_{z,1}\big)-\sum_{y\in Y}D\big(\fib{f}{u},p_z(\cdot,y)\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:miloss} to each slice and sum; the mass corrections
cancel by Lemma~\ref{lem:compat}(ii).
\end{proof}

Each $\Delta(z,u)$ is nonnegative by Lemma~\ref{lem:super}, so the loss is a sum
of local, individually nonnegative contributions, one per (context, fiber) pair:
every reported discrepancy has a return address.

\begin{theorem}[Fiberwise exactness criterion]\label{thm:tight}
$I(f(X);Y\mid Z)=I(X;Y\mid Z)$ if and only if $\Delta(z,u)=0$ for every context
$z$ and every fiber $u$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A sum of nonnegative terms vanishes iff each term does; combine with
Theorem~\ref{thm:cmiloss}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Local strictness]\label{thm:strictlocal}
If $\Delta(z_0,u_0)>0$ for a single pair, then $I(f(X);Y\mid Z)<I(X;Y\mid Z)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equality would force $\Delta(z_0,u_0)=0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:tight}, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} gives a strict inequality.
\end{proof}

\section{Classification of invisible collisions}

\begin{lemma}[Strict Gibbs, per term]\label{lem:gibbsterm}
Let $a,b\ge0$ with $b=0\Rightarrow a=0$ and $a\neq b$. Then
$\dfrac{a-b}{\ln 2}<a\big(\log_2 a-\log_2 b\big)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $a=0$ then $b>0$ and the left side is strictly negative while the right side
is $0$. If $a>0$ then $b>0$, and the claim is the strict form of $\ln t\le t-1$
at $t=b/a\neq1$, multiplied by $a/\ln2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Strict Gibbs]\label{lem:gibbs}
If $a,b:S\to\R_{\ge0}$ have equal total mass, $b$ is absolutely continuous for
$a$, and $a\neq b$ at some point, then
$\sum_{x\in S}a(x)\big(\log_2 a(x)-\log_2 b(x)\big)>0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Sum Lemma~\ref{lem:gibbsterm} over $S$, strictly at the point of difference and
weakly elsewhere; the linear terms sum to $(\sum a-\sum b)/\ln2=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict superadditivity]\label{thm:strictsuper}
Let $v_y:S\to\R_{\ge0}$, $w=\sum_y v_y$ and $W=\sum_{x\in S}w(x)>0$. If for some
$y_0$ and $x_0\in S$
\[
v_{y_0}(x_0)\;\neq\;\frac{w(x_0)\sum_{x\in S}v_{y_0}(x)}{W},
\]
then $\sum_y D(S,v_y)<D(S,w)$. Conversely, if $v_y(x)=w(x)\big(\sum_{x'}
v_y(x')\big)/W$ for all $y,x$, then $\sum_y D(S,v_y)=D(S,w)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expanding $D(S,v_y)=\sum_x v_y(x)\big(\log_2\sum_{x'}v_y(x')-\log_2 v_y(x)\big)$
and likewise for $w$, the difference $D(S,w)-\sum_y D(S,v_y)$ is a sum over $y$
of relative entropies of the column $v_y$ against the rank-one prediction
$x\mapsto w(x)\sum_{x'}v_y(x')/W$, which has the same column mass.
Lemma~\ref{lem:gibbs} gives strict positivity as soon as one cell deviates; the
converse is a direct cancellation of logarithms after substituting the product
form.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Classification of invisible collisions]\label{thm:classify}
Let $p\ge0$ and let $f$ be a label map. Then $I(f(X);Y\mid Z)=I(X;Y\mid Z)$ if
and only if for every context $z$ and every fiber $u$ of positive mass
$W_{z,u}=\sum_{x\in\fib{f}{u}}p_{z,1}(x)>0$, and all $y\in Y$,
$x\in\fib{f}{u}$,
\[
p_z(x,y)\;=\;\frac{p_{z,1}(x)\sum_{x'\in\fib{f}{u}}p_z(x',y)}{W_{z,u}} .
\]
Otherwise $I(f(X);Y\mid Z)<I(X;Y\mid Z)$ strictly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Fibers of zero mass contribute zero deficit on both sides; fibers
of positive mass satisfy the equality case of Theorem~\ref{thm:strictsuper}, so
$\Delta(z,u)=0$ throughout and Theorem~\ref{thm:tight} applies.
($\Rightarrow$) A violated cell gives $\Delta(z,u)>0$ by the strict half of
Theorem~\ref{thm:strictsuper}, whence a strict drop by
Theorem~\ref{thm:strictlocal}, contradicting equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Profile form]
The displayed condition says that inside a fiber, in a given context, the joint
table is rank one: every pooled dial setting has the \emph{same normalised
response profile}, namely the fiber-average profile. A collision is therefore
invisible to the conditional reading precisely when it pools dial settings that
are \emph{conditionally exchangeable}; otherwise the reading strictly
under-reports. There is no intermediate regime in which two sources of error
cancel.
\end{remark}

\section{An observable error bar}

\begin{definition}[Label entropy destroyed]
For a label map $f$, weight $p$ and context $z$,
\[
L_f(p,z)=H\big(X,p_{z,1}\big)-H\big(X',\push_f p_{z,1}\big)
=\sum_{u\in X'}D\big(\fib{f}{u},p_{z,1}\big)\;\ge\;0 .
\]
\end{definition}

The functional $L_f(p,z)$ depends only on the encoding $f$ and the dial
histogram $p_{z,1}$ within context $z$; it never inspects how the dial and the
response co-vary, which is exactly the data an auditor lacks.

\begin{theorem}[Error bar]\label{thm:errorbar}
For every nonnegative $p$ and every $f$,
\[
0\;\le\;I(X;Y\mid Z)-I(f(X);Y\mid Z)\;\le\;\sum_{z\in Z}L_f(p,z).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The lower bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi}. For the upper bound apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:cmiloss} and discard the nonnegative sliced deficits.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two-sided bracket]\label{cor:bracket}
$I(f(X);Y\mid Z)\le I(X;Y\mid Z)\le I(f(X);Y\mid Z)+\sum_z L_f(p,z)$: the true
value lies between two computable numbers.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Exactness without injectivity]\label{cor:exact}
If $L_f(p,z)=0$ for every context $z$ --- i.e.\ $f$ never pools two dial
settings that both occur with positive mass in the same context --- then
$I(f(X);Y\mid Z)=I(X;Y\mid Z)$ exactly, even though $f$ may be wildly
non-injective.
\end{corollary}

This strictly generalises Theorem~\ref{thm:inj}: injectivity is a global
hypothesis, whereas Corollary~\ref{cor:exact} only requires collisions to be
\emph{contextually inactive}. A collision between two settings deployed in
disjoint contexts costs nothing.

\begin{corollary}[Budget aggregation]
If $L_f(p,z)\le c$ for all $z$, the total loss is at most $|Z|\,c$.
\end{corollary}

The bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:errorbar} is attained; see
Section~\ref{sec:witnesses}.

\section{Structural properties}\label{sec:structure}

\subsection{Merges compose}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fibcomp}
For $f:X\to X'$, $g:X'\to X''$ and $w\in X''$, the sets
$\{x\in \fib{(g\circ f)}{w}: f(x)=u\}=\fib{f}{u}$, for $u\in \fib{g}{w}$,
partition $\fib{(g\circ f)}{w}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Composition]\label{thm:comp}
$g_*(f_*p)=(g\circ f)_*p$ for every weight $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides at $(w,y,z)$ sum $p(\cdot,y,z)$ over $\fib{(g\circ f)}{w}$; the left
side does so by first grouping along the fibers of $f$, legitimate by
Lemma~\ref{lem:fibcomp}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chained bugs are still one-sided]\label{thm:chain}
For all $f,g$ and nonnegative $p$,
\[
I\big(g(f(X));Y\mid Z\big)\;\le\;I\big(f(X);Y\mid Z\big)\;\le\;I(X;Y\mid Z).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Rewrite the left term via Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} as the reading of $g_*(f_*p)$
and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} to the nonnegative weight $f_*p$.
\end{proof}

Chaining is not a new phenomenon requiring new hypotheses: it is the same
operation with a composite label map. Errors accumulate monotonically; a second
defect can never cancel a first and push a reading above the truth.

\subsection{Sub-dials}

\begin{theorem}
Let the dial be compound, $X=X_1\times X_2$, and suppose only the first
coordinate is reported. Then $I(X_1;Y\mid Z)\le I\big((X_1,X_2);Y\mid Z\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The projection $X_1\times X_2\to X_1$ is a label map; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The capacity cap}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:margcap}
For nonnegative $q$, $H(Y,q_2)\le H(X\times Y,q)$, and consequently $I(q)\le
H(X,q_1)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $H(X\times Y,q)=\sum_y\sum_x\nlp(q(x,y))$ and use
$\nlp\big(\sum_x q(x,y)\big)\le\sum_x\nlp(q(x,y))$ columnwise, i.e.\
nonnegativity of the deficit.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Capacity cap]\label{thm:cap}
For nonnegative $p$,
\[
I(X;Y\mid Z)\;\le\;\sum_z\Big[H\big(X,p_{z,1}\big)-\nlp\big(\mass(p_z)\big)\Big]
\;=\;H(X\mid Z).
\]
\end{theorem}

Since a merge also lowers $H(X\mid Z)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:deficit}, a merged
table can never report more dependence than the label entropy surviving the
merge. Coarse tables are not merely biased downward; they are capped.

\subsection{The chain rule}

Write $p_{XZ}(x,z)=\sum_y p(x,y,z)$ for the context channel, and read $p$ itself
as the two-way table of $X$ against the pair $(Y,Z)$.

\begin{theorem}[Entropy balance and chain rule]\label{thm:chainrule}
For every weight $p$, with no normalisation,
\[
I(X;Y\mid Z)=H(X,Z)+H(Y,Z)-H(X,Y,Z)-H(Z),
\]
and consequently $I(X;Y\mid Z)=I\big(X;(Y,Z)\big)-I(X;Z)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Summing the four slice-wise terms over $z$ identifies each with a global
entropy: $\sum_z H(X,p_{z,1})=H(X\times Z,p_{XZ})$, $\sum_z H(Y,p_{z,2})=
H(Y\times Z,p_2)$, $\sum_z H(X\times Y,p_z)=H(X\times Y\times Z,p)$ and
$\sum_z\nlp(\mass(p_z))=H(Z,(p_{XZ})_2)$. For the second display expand
$I(X;(Y,Z))$ and $I(X;Z)$ and use $(p_{XZ})_1=p_1$, so that the dial marginal
cancels.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[A guarded comparison]\label{prop:guarded}
If $p\ge0$ and $\sum p\le1$, then $I(X;Y\mid Z)\le I(X;(Y,Z))$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:chainrule} the difference is $I(X;Z)$, and by
Proposition~\ref{prop:nonneg} $I(p_{XZ})\ge\nlp(\mass(p_{XZ}))$; the mass
hypothesis makes $\nlp(\mass)\ge0$.
\end{proof}

The hypothesis is necessary and marks a genuine boundary of the unnormalised
theory: for total mass exceeding $1$, $\nlp(\mass)$ turns negative and the
context reading can dip below zero. This is a normalisation defect, not a
failure of the underlying mathematics; every other result above is
normalisation-free.

\begin{remark}[Diagnostic reading]
The identity $I(X;(Y,Z))=I(X;Y\mid Z)+I(X;Z)$ says that a loud pair channel is
explained \emph{either} by genuine conditional dependence \emph{or} by
dial/context coupling, and never by both being small.
\end{remark}

\section{Sharpness, witnesses, and the boundary of the guarantee}
\label{sec:witnesses}

All witnesses live on $X=Y=Z=\{0,1\}$.

\subsection{Logical independence of conditional and unconditional readings}

\begin{example}[Parity population]
Put weight $1/4$ on each of the four cells with $z=x\oplus y$, and $0$
elsewhere. Its $X,Y$-marginal is uniform on all four cells, so $I(X;Y)=0$ bits.
Each of the two slices has mass $1/2$ and is supported on two diagonal cells of
weight $1/4$, giving slice reading $1-\tfrac12=\tfrac12$ bits; hence
$I(X;Y\mid Z)=1$ bit.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Copy population]
Put weight $1/2$ on each of the two cells with $x=y=z$. Its $X,Y$-marginal is
the diagonal with weights $1/2$, so $I(X;Y)=1$ bit; each slice is a single cell,
so $I(X;Y\mid Z)=0$.
\end{example}

Neither reading bounds the other: a guarantee about conditional columns cannot
be deduced from single-dial tables, nor conversely. The one-sidedness theorem
proved here is per-dial and conditional.

\subsection{The negative half is sharp}

Let $\mathrm{c}:\{0,1\}\to\{*\}$ be the collapsing merge. Applied to the parity
population it yields a one-row table with reading $0$ bits, whereas the truth is
$1$ bit. A reported conditional independence can therefore be a \emph{pure
artifact}: the merged table retains no trace of a maximal dependence.

\subsection{The error bar is attained}

For the same pair, the collapsing merge destroys exactly $\tfrac12$ bit of label
entropy in each of the two contexts, so $\sum_z L_{\mathrm{c}}(p,z)=1$ bit,
matching the loss exactly. Theorem~\ref{thm:errorbar} is therefore tight.

\subsection{The detection criterion fires}

In the context $z=1$ of the parity population, the fiber $\{0,1\}$ of the
collapsing merge is not a product block: the cell $(x,y)=(0,1)$ carries $1/4$
while the rank-one prediction from its row and column sums is
$(1/4)(1/4)/(1/2)=1/8$. Theorem~\ref{thm:classify} predicts a strict drop
independently of numerical evaluation.

\subsection{The chain rule, numerically}

For the parity population $I(X;(Y,Z))=1$ and $I(X;Z)=0$, whose difference $1$
matches the direct computation; for the copy population $I(X;(Y,Z))=1$ and
$I(X;Z)=1$, whose difference $0$ matches. Both independently confirm
Theorem~\ref{thm:chainrule}.

\subsection{The guarantee is specific to the dial axis}

Define the merge of the \emph{conditioning} variable by
$(f^{\sharp}p)(x,y,w)=\sum_{z:f(z)=w}p(x,y,z)$ and collapse the context.

\begin{theorem}[Two-sidedness of context merges]\label{thm:twosided}
There exist nonnegative weights $p,p'$ and a context merge $\mathrm{c}$ with
$I_{\mathrm{c}}(p)<I(p)$ and $I_{\mathrm{c}}(p')>I(p')$, where
$I_{\mathrm{c}}$ denotes the reading after collapsing the context. Hence no
analogue of Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} holds on the conditioning axis.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
On the parity population the reading falls from $1$ bit to $0$; on the copy
population it rises from $0$ to $1$ bit.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why the axes differ]
The asymmetry is legible in Theorem~\ref{thm:cmiloss}. A dial merge subtracts
sliced deficits from marginal deficits \emph{inside a fixed slice}, and the sign
is fixed by superadditivity (Lemma~\ref{lem:super}), i.e.\ by concavity of
entropy. A context merge instead \emph{recombines} slices, replacing several
slice readings by the reading of their sum; no convexity constrains the
direction of that recombination.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms and audit procedures}

Let $n=|X|$, $m=|Y|$, $k=|Z|$, $n'=|X'|$.

\begin{description}[leftmargin=1.6em,style=nextline]
\item[A. Reading computation.] Compute $I(X;Y\mid Z)$ from a three-way table in
$O(nmk)$ time and $O(nm)$ working space: for each context, form both marginals
and the joint entropy in one pass over the slice.
\item[B. Artifact localisation.] Given the true table and a merge $f$, compute
all $\Delta(z,u)$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:cmiloss} in $O(nmk)$ time and report the
(context, fiber) pairs sorted by contribution. Since the terms are individually
nonnegative and sum exactly to the loss, this is a complete attribution.
\item[C. Audit bracket.] Given only the merged table and the per-context dial
histograms, compute the bracket of Corollary~\ref{cor:bracket} in $O(nk+n'mk)$
time and label each conditional cell \emph{certified dependent},
\emph{inconclusive with bound $b$}, or \emph{certified exact}. This procedure
needs no access to the true joint table.
\item[D. Exchangeability test.] For each context and fiber, test the rank-one
condition of Theorem~\ref{thm:classify} to numerical tolerance in $O(nmk)$ total
time. A failure certifies a strictly low reading; a global pass certifies
exactness.
\end{description}

\section{Discussion}

The result is best understood as a \emph{sign} statement rather than an accuracy
statement, and sign statements have different robustness properties. An accuracy
bound degrades when its modelling assumptions fail, often silently. The
guarantee here survives arbitrary coarsening, arbitrary chaining
(Theorem~\ref{thm:chain}), arbitrarily bad encodings and unnormalised count
data, because it does not depend on how bad any of these are. Its practical
analogue is a diagnostic test with no false positives: possibly insensitive, of
unknown sensitivity, but a positive result is proof.

The three layers fit together as follows. Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} fixes the sign;
Theorem~\ref{thm:errorbar} fixes the size in observable terms;
Theorem~\ref{thm:classify} fixes the condition under which the error is zero.
Together they say that a merged table supports the inference ``dependent''
unconditionally, supports ``exact'' whenever the fiberwise exchangeability test
passes, and otherwise supports ``at least this dependent, and at most this much
more.''

The theory is finite and combinatorial, and nothing assumes normalisation except
Proposition~\ref{prop:guarded}. The guarantee is per-dial and conditional;
Section~\ref{sec:witnesses} shows this is a feature of the truth rather than of
the proof, since the conditional and unconditional readings are logically
independent and merges of the conditioning variable are two-sided.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \textbf{Profile form of the detection criterion.} The product condition of
Theorem~\ref{thm:classify} should be equivalent to the operational statement
that all dial settings inside a fiber have the same normalised response profile
in that context; hence a strict drop occurs exactly when some fiber contains two
positive-mass settings whose profiles differ. The rank-one prediction is
precisely the assertion ``profile of $x$ equals the fiber-average profile'', so
the classification already proved should yield this as a corollary.
\item \textbf{Quantitative detection.} A version of
Theorem~\ref{thm:classify} bounding the drop from below in terms of a distance
between profiles would convert a qualitative certificate into a lower bound on
the missing information, closing the audit bracket from the other side.
\item \textbf{Sharper error bars.} Theorem~\ref{thm:errorbar} discards all
sliced deficits, which is lossy in typical cases. A bound retaining a computable
proxy for them --- based on the merged table's own column structure --- would
tighten the bracket while remaining computable without the true table.
\item \textbf{Normalisation.} Proposition~\ref{prop:guarded} fails for total
mass exceeding $1$ because $\nlp(\mass)$ changes sign. A systematically
normalised variant of the theory would remove the one hypothesis that is not
intrinsic.
\item \textbf{Context-axis structure.} Theorem~\ref{thm:twosided} rules out a
sign guarantee for context merges but not a two-sided bound on how far a context
merge can move a reading, in terms of an observable of the context encoding.
\item \textbf{Randomised label maps.} Extending the exact accounting of
Theorem~\ref{thm:cmiloss} to stochastic label maps would cover sampling and
privacy mechanisms; the expected form of the answer replaces fiber deficits by
conditional deficits of the channel.
\end{enumerate}

